criterion <id> returns that criterion's record. criterion --of <feature-id> returns every criterion beneath one feature, and criterion --tag <qualifier> every criterion carrying one qualifier. The answer is a YAML mapping whose one key, criteria, holds a list of the matching records in every case, one identifier included; --json writes the same values as JSON. An identifier no criterion declares is reported as such and exits non-zero. criterion --changed compares the current corpus, uncommitted edits included, against the corpus at the merge-base of HEAD and the repository's default branch, or of HEAD and the revision --since names, and lists every acceptance criterion whose content hash differs, classifying each as changed, added or removed. Its answer is a YAML mapping holding base (the revision and the merge-base commit) and then criteria; it exits 0 whatever it lists, or 1 on a non-empty list under --exit-code. It needs git on PATH.

Usage:
  specs criterion [<id>] [flags]

Flags:
      --changed        list acceptance criteria whose content differs from the corpus at a git base
      --exit-code      with --changed, exit 1 when any criterion is listed
  -h, --help           help for criterion
      --json           write the answer as JSON instead of YAML
      --of string      list every criterion beneath this feature
      --since string   with --changed, compare against the merge-base of HEAD and this branch, tag or commit instead of the default branch
      --tag string     list every criterion carrying this qualifier

Global Flags:
      --config string   path to specs.toml (default: the nearest specs.toml in the working directory or above it, up to the repository root)
